---

<LessonPage page={page}>
<main class="wrap" data-core-duration="60–70 minutes" data-extension-duration="15–20 minutes">
  <p class="kicker"><span class="dot"></span> Grammar · Lesson {page.sequence}</p>
  <h1>Defining relative clauses: <em>attach the clue to the noun</em></h1>
  <p class="standfirst">A defining relative clause helps a listener identify the exact person, thing, place, or possession you mean. First name a general category, then attach the essential clue directly to it: <span class="ex">the analyst who called</span>, <span class="ex">the file that you sent</span>, <span class="ex">the room where we met</span>.</p>
  <div class="meta-row">
    <span class="tag">60–70 min core</span>
    <span class="tag">noun + identifying clause</span>
    <span class="tag">subject gap · object gap · zero relative</span>
    <span class="tag">15–20 min listening and retrieval</span>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson-flow" aria-label="Lesson flow">
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Notice</b><span>Find the noun and the essential clue.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Discover</b><span>Locate the missing role inside the clause.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Connect</b><span>Choose, keep, or omit the relative word.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Use</b><span>Describe a precise fictional shortlist.</span></div>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line"><b>Can-do:</b> By the end, you can attach an essential identifying clause directly to its noun; use <span class="ex">who</span>, <span class="ex">which</span>, <span class="ex">that</span>, <span class="ex">whose</span>, and <span class="ex">where</span> accurately; distinguish subject and object gaps; omit an object relative word when the structure allows it; keep subject-verb agreement with the noun being identified; avoid repeated pronouns, misplaced clauses, redundant place prepositions, and <span class="ex">what</span> after an expressed noun; recognize more formal preposition patterns; and deliver a connected recommendation whose references remain clear.</p>

  <h2>Two-minute retrieval</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You recovered a person question, a place question, and the subject pronoun she before replacing repeated information with a relative clause.">
    <h3>Reactivate the reference tools</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Relative clause foundation choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="who">who</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="where">where</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="she">she</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="what">what</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="it">it</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Ask for a person’s identity: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="who" data-hint="Choose the question word that asks about a person." data-fix="Who manages the project?">choose</button> manages the project?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Ask for a location: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="where" data-hint="Choose the question word that asks about a place." data-fix="Where does the team meet?">choose</button> does the team meet?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Replace <span class="ex">Marta</span> with a subject pronoun: Marta called. <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="she" data-hint="Marta is one woman and performs the action called." data-fix="Marta called. She left a message.">choose</button> left a message.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Notice how one clause narrows a category</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Shortlist for a community workshop</h3>
    <p><b>Coordinator:</b> Nia. <b>Goal:</b> choose one trainer, one projector, and one room from several possible options.</p>
    <p><span class="ex">Let’s invite the trainer who teaches evening classes.</span></p>
    <p><span class="ex">Let’s reserve the projector that Omar tested yesterday.</span></p>
    <p><span class="ex">Let’s use the room where the large tables are stored.</span></p>
    <p><span class="ex">Let’s call the organization whose volunteers ran last year’s workshop.</span></p>
    <ul>
      <li>What general noun comes before each identifying clause?</li>
      <li>Does <span class="ex">who</span> perform an action, or does another subject follow it?</li>
      <li>Does <span class="ex">that</span> perform <span class="ex">tested</span>, or does Omar?</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You found each noun, matched the reference type, and distinguished the subject gap after trainer from the object gap after projector.">
    <h3>Discovery check: trace the reference</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Defining relative clause discovery choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="trainer">trainer</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="a person">a person</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="who">who</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Omar">Omar</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="the organization’s volunteers">the organization’s volunteers</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>In <span class="ex">the trainer who teaches evening classes</span>, the clause identifies the noun <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="trainer" data-hint="Look immediately before who." data-fix="The clause who teaches evening classes identifies trainer.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The relative word <span class="ex">who</span> tells the listener that the noun refers to <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="a person" data-hint="Trainer refers to a human being in this context." data-fix="Who introduces an identifying clause about a person.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>In that first clause, <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="who" data-hint="No other subject appears before teaches." data-fix="Who is the subject of teaches.">choose</button> performs the action <span class="ex">teaches</span>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>In <span class="ex">the projector that Omar tested</span>, <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Omar" data-hint="The named person directly before tested performs the action." data-fix="Omar is the subject of tested; that represents the projector as object.">choose</button> performs <span class="ex">tested</span>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>In <span class="ex">the organization whose volunteers ran the workshop</span>, <span class="ex">whose volunteers</span> means <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="the organization’s volunteers" data-hint="Whose expresses the relationship between the organization and its volunteers." data-fix="Whose volunteers means the organization’s volunteers.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Get to the root: the clause contains one open role</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>Start with two ideas that repeat the same referent: <span class="ex">We need a trainer. The trainer teaches evenings.</span> Join them by placing the identifying information after the noun and replacing the repeated role: <span class="ex">We need a trainer who teaches evenings.</span> Inside <span class="ex">who teaches evenings</span>, the subject role is now filled by <span class="ex">who</span>.</p>
  <p>With an object relative, another subject remains: <span class="ex">Omar tested the projector</span> becomes <span class="ex">the projector that Omar tested</span>. Omar still performs the action. The projector is the unspoken object after <span class="ex">tested</span>. Do not add another <span class="ex">it</span>.</p>

  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>reference</th><th>common defining pattern</th><th>complete example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>person</td><td><span class="ex">who / that</span></td><td><span class="ex">the trainer who teaches evenings</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>thing</td><td><span class="ex">which / that</span></td><td><span class="ex">the projector that Omar tested</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>possession or association</td><td><span class="ex">whose + noun</span></td><td><span class="ex">the group whose volunteers helped</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>place</td><td><span class="ex">where + clause</span></td><td><span class="ex">the room where we store the tables</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p><span class="ex">That</span> is common with people and things in defining clauses. <span class="ex">Which</span> is also grammatical for things in defining clauses, although some U.S. editorial styles prefer <span class="ex">that</span>. Choose by grammar, clarity, and the style you are using, not by a false rule that defining <span class="ex">which</span> is impossible.</p>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You placed every clause beside its noun, preserved the real subject, and left exactly one represented role inside each relative clause.">
    <h3>Clause anatomy: noun, subject, and open role</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Relative clause anatomy choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="who">who</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="that">that</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="the analyst">the analyst</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="we">we</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="directly after the noun">directly after the noun</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="no repeated object">no repeated object</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>A person performed <span class="ex">called</span>: The analyst <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="who" data-hint="Choose the person relative that can be the subject of called." data-fix="The analyst who called left a message.">choose</button> called left a message.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>In that sentence, the grammatical subject of <span class="ex">called</span> refers back to <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="the analyst" data-hint="Who carries the analyst reference into the clause." data-fix="The analyst is the person who performed called.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>A thing received the action: The form <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="that" data-hint="Choose the defining relative for a thing; the subject we follows." data-fix="The form that we submitted was accepted.">choose</button> we submitted was accepted.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>Inside <span class="ex">that we submitted</span>, <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="we" data-hint="The pronoun before submitted performs the action." data-fix="We is the subject; that represents the form as object.">choose</button> performs the action.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>For clear attachment, put the identifying clause <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="directly after the noun" data-hint="The listener should meet the clue immediately after the noun it identifies." data-fix="Place the relative clause directly after the noun it identifies.">choose</button> it identifies.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>Because <span class="ex">that</span> already represents <span class="ex">the form</span>, the clause <span class="ex">that we submitted</span> needs <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="no repeated object" data-hint="Do not add it after submitted." data-fix="Say the form that we submitted, with no repeated object.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Choose a relative word from the reference you need</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p><span class="ex">Who</span> is the neutral everyday choice for a person. <span class="ex">Which</span> refers to a thing. <span class="ex">That</span> can refer to either in a defining clause. <span class="ex">Whose</span> comes before a noun and expresses possession or a close association. <span class="ex">Where</span> means <span class="ex">in / at that place</span>.</p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You matched people, things, possession, and place while keeping agreement with the noun each clause identifies.">
    <h3>Reference check: use the only option that completes this sentence</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Relative word choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="who">who</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="which">which</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="that">that</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="whose">whose</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="where">where</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="works">works</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="work">work</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The sentence must use the person-specific option, not the more general <span class="ex">that</span>: The tutor <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="who" data-hint="The prompt asks for the relative word specifically associated with a person." data-fix="The tutor who leads the workshop speaks Spanish.">choose</button> leads the workshop speaks Spanish.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The sentence must use the thing-specific option, not <span class="ex">that</span>: The tablet <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="which" data-hint="The prompt asks for the relative word specifically associated with a thing." data-fix="The tablet which has the blue case belongs to Nia.">choose</button> has the blue case belongs to Nia.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Use the defining relative that works for either a person or a thing: The projector <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="that" data-hint="That is the shared defining option for people and things." data-fix="The projector that Omar tested is available.">choose</button> Omar tested is available.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The volunteers belong to the organization: Choose the organization <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="whose" data-hint="A noun, volunteers, follows the blank and belongs to the organization." data-fix="Choose the organization whose volunteers have first-aid training.">choose</button> volunteers have first-aid training.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The room is the location of the storage action: Use the room <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="where" data-hint="Where carries the meaning in the room." data-fix="Use the room where we store the large tables.">choose</button> we store the large tables.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>One tutor agrees with a singular head noun: The tutor who <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="works" data-hint="The head noun tutor is singular, so the clause verb needs -s." data-fix="The tutor who works evenings is available.">choose</button> evenings is available.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">7</span><span>Several tutors agree with a plural head noun: The tutors who <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="work" data-hint="The head noun tutors is plural, so use the base form work." data-fix="The tutors who work evenings are available.">choose</button> evenings are available.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Keep a subject relative; an object relative may disappear</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>Use the first word after the relative marker as a quick diagnostic. If a finite verb comes next, the relative word is usually the subject and cannot be omitted: <span class="ex">the trainer who teaches evenings</span>. If a separate subject comes next, the relative word is the object and can often be omitted: <span class="ex">the trainer who Nia recommended</span> or <span class="ex">the trainer Nia recommended</span>.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>inside the clause</th><th>role</th><th>omission</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">who teaches evenings</span></td><td><span class="ex">who</span> is subject</td><td>keep it</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">that Nia recommended</span></td><td><span class="ex">Nia</span> is subject; <span class="ex">that</span> is object</td><td><span class="ex">that</span> may be omitted</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>For a person as object, <span class="ex">who</span> is normal in conversation: <span class="ex">the trainer who Nia recommended</span>. <span class="ex">Whom</span> is more formal and survives especially after a fronted preposition. It is not necessary for the everyday object pattern practiced here.</p>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You kept every subject relative, recognized a separate clause subject, and omitted only object relatives.">
    <h3>Omission check: inspect what comes next</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Relative word omission choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="must stay">must stay</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="may be omitted">may be omitted</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="the coordinator">the coordinator</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="the projector">the projector</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>In <span class="ex">the trainer who teaches evenings</span>, <span class="ex">who</span> performs <span class="ex">teaches</span>, so it <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="must stay" data-hint="A subject relative cannot disappear because the clause would have no subject." data-fix="Keep who: the trainer who teaches evenings.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>In <span class="ex">the trainer who Nia recommended</span>, Nia performs <span class="ex">recommended</span>, so <span class="ex">who</span> <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="may be omitted" data-hint="A separate subject, Nia, follows; who is the object." data-fix="Both the trainer who Nia recommended and the trainer Nia recommended are grammatical.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>In <span class="ex">the projector that stopped working</span>, <span class="ex">that</span> <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="must stay" data-hint="No separate subject appears before stopped." data-fix="Keep that: the projector that stopped working.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>In <span class="ex">the projector that the coordinator reserved</span>, <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="the coordinator" data-hint="The noun phrase after that performs reserved." data-fix="The coordinator is the subject of reserved.">choose</button> performs <span class="ex">reserved</span>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>In that same clause, <span class="ex">that</span> represents <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="the projector" data-hint="The reserved thing is the projector named before the clause." data-fix="That represents the projector as the object of reserved.">choose</button> as the object.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>Therefore, <span class="ex">the projector the coordinator reserved</span> is possible because object <span class="ex">that</span> <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="may be omitted" data-hint="The clause keeps its subject the coordinator after omission." data-fix="That may be omitted in the projector the coordinator reserved.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Control <span class="ex">whose</span>, <span class="ex">where</span>, and <span class="ex">what</span></h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p><span class="ex">Whose</span> is followed by the possessed or associated noun: <span class="ex">the group whose volunteers helped</span>. It can refer to a person, organization, and sometimes a thing when the relationship is natural. <span class="ex">Where</span> already contains a place relationship: <span class="ex">the room where we met</span> means <span class="ex">the room in which we met</span>.</p>
  <p><span class="ex">What</span> does not normally follow an expressed noun in this pattern. It already means something like <span class="ex">the thing that</span>: say <span class="ex">I understood what Nia meant</span>, but <span class="ex">the point that Nia made</span>. Do not say <span class="cross">the point what Nia made</span>.</p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You used whose before the associated noun, treated where as a place relationship, and kept what away from an already expressed noun.">
    <h3>Boundary check: do not express the same relationship twice</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Whose where and what choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="whose schedule">whose schedule</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="where">where</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="that">that</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="what">what</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="that we work in">that we work in</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Identify a tutor through that tutor’s schedule: We need a tutor <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="whose schedule" data-hint="Whose must come before the noun schedule." data-fix="We need a tutor whose schedule includes Saturdays.">choose</button> includes Saturdays.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Use one complete place relationship with no final preposition: This is the room <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="where" data-hint="Where already means in that room here." data-fix="This is the room where we work.">choose</button> we work.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Use the alternative pattern that keeps <span class="ex">in</span> with the verb: This is the room <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="that we work in" data-hint="When that introduces the room as object of in, retain the preposition at the end." data-fix="This is the room that we work in.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>An expressed noun, <span class="ex">point</span>, comes before the clause: I understood the point <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="that" data-hint="Use a relative marker after the expressed noun point." data-fix="I understood the point that Nia made.">choose</button> Nia made.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>No noun comes before the clause, and the meaning is <span class="ex">the thing that</span>: I understood <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="what" data-hint="What can carry the meaning the thing that when no noun is expressed first." data-fix="I understood what Nia meant.">choose</button> Nia meant.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Essential identification usually has no comma break</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>A defining clause is part of the noun phrase that identifies the referent. In writing, do not separate it with commas: <span class="ex">The volunteers who completed the safety training can supervise the room.</span> This identifies a subset of the volunteers. Compare <span class="ex">Our four volunteers, who completed the safety training, can supervise the room.</span> The commas present the clause as extra information about all four. That non-defining pattern is developed at B2.</p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You kept the essential clue attached without commas and interpreted it as selecting a subset rather than describing everyone.">
    <h3>Information check: which people does the clause select?</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Defining information choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="no commas">no commas</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="only the volunteers who completed the training">only the volunteers who completed the training</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="the clause is needed to identify the trainer">the clause is needed to identify the trainer</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>For the essential identifying meaning, write <span class="ex">The volunteers who completed the training can supervise</span> with <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="no commas" data-hint="A defining clause stays integrated with its noun in writing." data-fix="Use no commas around who completed the training.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>That sentence gives permission to <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="only the volunteers who completed the training" data-hint="The clause selects the trained subset of volunteers." data-fix="Only the volunteers who completed the training can supervise.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>In <span class="ex">Please call the trainer who teaches evenings</span>, the listener needs the clause because <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="the clause is needed to identify the trainer" data-hint="There is more than one possible trainer, and the schedule distinguishes one." data-fix="The clause identifies which trainer the listener should call.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Place prepositions where the register expects them</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>Everyday English often leaves the preposition at the end: <span class="ex">the person I spoke to</span>, <span class="ex">the room that we met in</span>. More formal English can move the preposition before <span class="ex">whom</span> or <span class="ex">which</span>: <span class="ex">the person to whom I spoke</span>, <span class="ex">the room in which we met</span>. Do not put <span class="ex">that</span> after a fronted preposition: <span class="cross">the room in that we met</span>.</p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You matched everyday preposition stranding with zero relative, and formal fronted prepositions with whom or which rather than that.">
    <h3>Register check: preserve the requested style</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Relative clause preposition choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="I spoke to">I spoke to</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="to whom I spoke">to whom I spoke</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="in which we met">in which we met</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Natural conversation with the object relative omitted: Nia is the coordinator <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="I spoke to" data-hint="Leave to with spoke in the requested conversational pattern." data-fix="Nia is the coordinator I spoke to.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Formal written version with the preposition first: Nia is the coordinator <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="to whom I spoke" data-hint="After fronted to, use formal object whom for a person." data-fix="Nia is the coordinator to whom I spoke.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Formal written place pattern: The studio <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="in which we met" data-hint="Use in plus which for the room relationship in the requested formal style." data-fix="The studio in which we met has moved.">choose</button> has moved.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Sound natural: make the noun and clause one spoken unit</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>In a defining phrase, do not announce the relative clause with a strong comma-like pause. Keep the noun and identifying clue in one rhythm group: <span class="ex">the TRAINer who TEACHes EVEning classes</span>. The relative word is usually unstressed because the important information is the noun and the new clue.</p>
  <p>In conversation, object relatives often lose the relative word completely: <span class="ex">the PROjector Omar TESTed</span>. Link the noun into the following subject rather than inserting a pause. When <span class="ex">that</span> remains, it may be weak /ðət/: <span class="ex">the FILE that you SENT</span>. Keep subject <span class="ex">who</span> or <span class="ex">that</span> audible enough to preserve the clause structure.</p>

  <section data-lesson-extension="Tutor-read listening for reference, omission, stress, rhythm, and preposition register">
